# Line endings are pinned to LF everywhere, in the repository and in the
# working copy.
#
# This is not tidiness. The sibling project has a written record of two tests
# that passed on Linux and in CI and failed on a Windows machine, because a
# helper split on "\n" and every line but the last kept a trailing "\r". The
# failure read `'pcm_s16le\r' !== 'pcm_s16le'`, which looks like a codec fault
# and is a line-ending fault, and CI running ubuntu-latest would never have
# caught it.
#
# Nothing in this repository is parsed by a Windows shell — the scripts are
# POSIX shell and Node, deliberately, so there is no file that needs CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary

# The one exception, and it follows from the same reasoning as the rule.
#
# These are sample exports from Qualtrics, SPSS and Google Forms, kept because
# the parser has to survive what those tools actually write — and what they
# write is CRLF, with a byte-order mark on the Qualtrics one. Normalised to LF
# on checkout they would still parse, and would silently stop testing the thing
# they exist to test: a fixture that has been cleaned up is a fixture that
# proves nothing about dirty input.
#
# `-text` rather than `eol=crlf`, so git does no conversion in either
# direction and the bytes in a fresh clone are the bytes that were committed.
supabase/functions/_shared/research/analytics/fixtures/*.csv -text
